    text: "Beverages — or drinks — are an essential part of daily life, social gatherings, and cultural traditions. Water is the most fundamental drink, available as still (flat) or sparkling (fizzy). Hot beverages dominate morning routines: coffee — from a simple black coffee to elaborate lattes, cappuccinos, and espressos — fuels millions of people daily. Tea is equally global, ranging from classic black tea and green tea to herbal infusions like chamomile, peppermint, and ginger tea. Hot chocolate provides a comforting alternative in cold weather. Soft drinks include fizzy drinks (carbonated beverages) like cola and lemonade, as well as fruit juices — orange juice, apple juice, and tropical blends. Smoothies, made from blended fruit and yoghurt, have become a health-conscious favourite. Energy drinks and sports drinks target active consumers. Milk — whether cow's, oat, almond, or soya — is both a drink and a cooking ingredient. Alcoholic beverages form their own vast category, though many people choose non-alcoholic alternatives. Understanding beverage vocabulary is essential for ordering in cafés and restaurants, shopping in supermarkets, reading menus, and discussing preferences — skills that arise in almost every social and professional situation in English.",
